            <ArticleTitle>Acute Painful Ptosis Secondary to IgG4 Dacryoadenitis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>108-11</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1159/000444164</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 48-year-old lorry driver presented with 3 weeks of blurred vision, pain and diplopia. There was a right upper lid ptosis with some restriction of eye movements. A CT revealed an enlarged lacrimal gland and lacrimal gland biopsy showed IgG4-positive plasma cells. The patient responded to oral prednisolone and fully recovered. As a condition which mimics a number of diseases, an IgG4-related disease presents a diagnostic challenge and ought to be considered in both acute and chronic presentations.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
